            "text": "{\"primary_meaning\": \"God's moral order applied to human relationships, characterized by restorative action, equitable treatment, and the active protection of the vulnerable (such as widows, orphans, and the poor).\", \"primary_basis\": [\"Amos 5:24\", \"Micah 6:8\", \"Isaiah 1:17\", \"Psalm 89:14\"], \"alternatives\": [{\"meaning\": \"Imputed righteousness or justification, where God legally declares sinners just through the atonement of Christ (often translating the Greek dikaiosyne).\", \"basis\": [\"Romans 3:24-26\", \"2 Corinthians 5:21\"], \"prominence\": \"dominant\"}, {\"meaning\": \"Retributive justice and divine judgment against unrepentant sin and wickedness.\", \"basis\": [\"Romans 12:19\", \"Revelation 19:2\", \"Deuteronomy 32:4\"], \"prominence\": \"common\"}, {\"meaning\": \"Liberation and socio-economic restructuring, emphasizing the overthrow of systemic oppression (prominent in liberation theology).\", \"basis\": [\"Luke 4:18-19\", \"Exodus 3:7-9\", \"Matthew 25:31-46\"], \"prominence\": \"common\"}], \"has_settled_consensus\": true, \"confidence\": \"high\", \"notes\": \"In biblical Hebrew, 'justice' (mishpat) and 'righteousness' (tsedaqah) are frequently paired, indicating that biblical justice goes beyond strict legal retribution to include restorative action and social equity. In the New Testament, the Greek term 'dikaiosyne' encompasses both personal righteousness and God's justifying justice.\"}",
